# Day 13 – Linux Volume Management (LVM)

## 🎯 Goal

Learn how to create, manage, mount, and extend storage using **LVM**.

## 🔧 Commands Used

### 1. Check Storage

```bash
lsblk
pvs
vgs
lvs
df -h
```

### 2. Create Physical Volume

```bash
pvcreate /dev/sdb
pvs
```

### 3. Create Volume Group

```bash
vgcreate devops-vg /dev/sdb
vgs
```

### 4. Create Logical Volume

```bash
lvcreate -L 500M -n app-data devops-vg
lvs
```

### 5. Format & Mount

```bash
mkfs.ext4 /dev/devops-vg/app-data
mkdir -p /mnt/app-data
mount /dev/devops-vg/app-data /mnt/app-data
df -h /mnt/app-data
```

### 6. Extend the Volume

```bash
lvextend -L +200M /dev/devops-vg/app-data
resize2fs /dev/devops-vg/app-data
df -h /mnt/app-data
```

## 🧠 What I Learned

* **PV (Physical Volume)** is the storage device used by LVM.
* **VG (Volume Group)** combines one or more PVs into a storage pool.
* **LV (Logical Volume)** is the flexible storage volume created from a VG.
* LVM makes it easier to **extend storage without replacing the disk**.

## 📌 Key Takeaway

LVM provides flexible storage management by allowing volumes to be **created, mounted, and extended easily**.
